  "slug": "prediction-multi-ec3e",
  "summary": "Recent journal and preprint work extends graph and hypergraph learning to sparse biomedical association and interaction data with structure-aware designs.",
  "synthesis": "Across these items, the common thread is graph-structured representation learning applied to pairwise and higher-order biomedical associations, including drug-drug, drug-target, protein-protein, lncRNA-disease, miRNA-disease, herb-symptom, and gene regulatory relations. Methods combine graph attention, hypergraphs, graph autoencoders, contrastive or curriculum learning, and Kolmogorov-Arnold networks, often paired with adaptive feature or structure selection to handle sparse data. What is new is structure-aware design: the circRNA-miRNA framework adaptively adjusts multi-relational hypergraph structure, and Motif-GRN builds motif-based hypergraphs for gene regulatory network inference. Reported results include AUROC 0.9819 for HMVA-DDI on DrugBank and about a 4% micro-F1 gain with 76% less memory for JCCLPPI. The dominant thread is methodological, and superiority claims remain specific to the benchmarks each paper reports.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-10-01"
}
